# The HOST crontab: ONE line per job. All locking, logging and evidence lines
# live inside the verb — the cron line carries no flock and no redirect, so it
# can never drift per host again. This is shared/crontab.example's charter,
# applied to the other side of the fleet, and it is not hypothetical there:
# five hand-edited variants of the on-box line drifted before that file was
# written, and a skipped tick that wrote nothing made a wedged bot
# indistinguishable from a healthy quiet one.
#
# ONE job is scheduled. That is a deliberate subtraction and not an omission;
# the block below the line is where it is argued, and it is where a reader
# who came here to add a second line should start.
#
# Install with `crontab -e` on the box HOST (not in a box). $HOME is expanded
# by cron; the path is the link `install.sh` maintains.
#
# Daily — drain and cycle every roster box. A busy box is SKIPPED by name and
# never forced; the run exits 3 when any box was skipped, and nothing is lost.
# It is also the fleet's only routine liveness signal: grok-reviewer ran no
# session at all between 2026-08-19 and 2026-08-28, and nothing on the board
# or on the floor reported it.
10 4 * * * $HOME/.local/bin/crew restart --all
#
# THE WEEKLY `crew reset --all` IS DEFERRED TO 0.1.4, AND IT IS NOT LEFT HERE
# COMMENTED OUT ON PURPOSE. A commented job line is an invitation to reinstate
# a defect by uncommenting, in the one file nobody diffs. If you want it back,
# read #328 first and bring it back with the flow that makes it safe.
#
# Why it is not scheduled (#678). The reset restores every roster box to its
# `armed` checkpoint, and that is a whole-VM snapshot restore with no carve-out
# for $DUTY_DIR. Most of what lives there survives being lost — the .seen-*
# ledgers are cost-only and self-healing, mentions are made idempotent
# GitHub-side, and declines are re-read from the board. Two things are not:
#
#   duty.log is the fleet's only record of its own sessions. It carries every
#   SESSION END line and #469's tier, token and cost fields, and it lives
#   inside the wiped directory — so a weekly restore leaves the fleet holding
#   at most seven days of its own history, in the very window that ships
#   telemetry into that file.
#
#   The resume breaker is cleared with no push. #314's invariant, written
#   after the #311 flood, is that ONLY A PUSH CLEARS IT; a restore clears it
#   anyway, so every correctly-suppressed lane re-arms each Sunday and buys
#   itself three more dispatches before it re-suppresses.
#
# Neither side of that is wrong alone: the schedule is right about disk and
# liveness, the breaker is right about floods. What nobody had written down is
# that one of them is a durability assumption the other violates.
#
# What returns it: #328, the collector, moves that evidence off-box. Once it
# is durable a restore costs nothing that matters, and the weekly line comes
# back as part of that flow rather than as an edit here. The reasoning for why
# the two verbs differ at all — price and period, @danmt 2026-08-29 — is kept
# in shared/docs/host-maintenance.md, because that argument is what this
# deferral gets reversed from.
#
# `crew reset` ITSELF IS UNCHANGED and remains available on demand: the verb,
# its version interlock, its refusals, the host lock and the job log are all
# exactly as #590 shipped them. It is still the right thing to run by hand
# after a `crew upgrade`, and still what reclaims a box that has really
# filled. Only the schedule changed.
#
# What the deferral costs until 0.1.4, said here because it is real and
# currently unowned: the weekly reset had become the fleet's de-facto garbage
# collector, and nothing else removes the detached review worktrees (#606).
# The daily restart clears /tmp and week-old guest state; it does not remove
# those. The runbook names the reading an operator should take.
#
# Read the evidence in the host job log, not in cron mail:
#   ~/.local/state/crew/host-maintenance.log
# Every run writes a start line and an end line there, including a run that
# did nothing and a run that was skipped by the lock — so silence in that log
# at a boundary means exactly one thing: cron itself is dead.
#
# There is no second line. The weekly reset is deferred, above; the real-host
# drill is operator-attended by construction and is deliberately not scheduled
# here either.
